1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:22,199
HOST: Columbus was wrong about almost everything. His math shrank the Earth to make Asia look like a short sail west. The experts checked his numbers and said no.   Spain paid for the voyage anyway. Three small ships, into the unmapped Atlantic. Why would a king and queen fund a plan the experts rejected?

2
00:00:22,199 --> 00:00:39,200
HOST: By the end: One: the economic pressures that pushed Iberian rulers toward a western route to Asia. Two: how the Reconquista and new ships made sustained Atlantic voyages possible. Three: a ranking of gold, God, and glory, defended with evidence.

3
00:00:39,200 --> 00:01:04,200
HOST: Start with hunger. Not for food. For pepper. After the Crusades, Europe craved Asian spices, silk, and perfumes. But every bale traveled overland through Islamic powers and Italian city-states, and each middleman took a cut. In Lisbon, prices had multiplied.  A sea route straight to Asia would skip every taxman. Whoever found it would command the richest trade on Earth.

4
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:38,400
HOST: Portugal moved first. In the mid-1400s, Prince Henry the Navigator sponsored voyage after voyage down the coast of Africa. The tool was the caravel, a small Portuguese ship with lateen sails that could tack against the wind, guided by the astrolabe and compass.  In 1488, Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope. A decade later, Vasco da Gama reached India: the sea route built one careful voyage at a time.  A Portuguese captain rounding the cape might have logged it like this:

5
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:48,400
NAVIGATOR: The cape lies behind us. The sea road east lies open. What waits beyond, no European knows.

6
00:01:48,400 --> 00:02:16,599
HOST: Spain came late, because Spain was busy. For nearly eight centuries, Christian kingdoms fought the Reconquista, retaking Iberia from Muslim rule. In 1492, Granada fell. The war was over. Spain stood united under Ferdinand and Isabella, holding a crusading army with nowhere left to march.  That crusading spirit did not retire. It turned outward: strike at Islam's flank, carry the faith across the ocean, win glory doing it.

7
00:02:16,599 --> 00:02:53,599
HOST: Enter Columbus: a Genoese sailor with a wrong map and a gift for persuasion. He argued the Earth was smaller than the experts said, which put Asia within reach. He asked Portugal first. Portugal, confident in its African route, turned him down.  Then he asked Spain. Ferdinand and Isabella had just united the country and were flush with victory. The price was tiny: three small ships. On October 12, 1492, he sighted land, an island he named San Salvador, and called its people Indians. He believed he had reached Asia.

8
00:02:53,599 --> 00:03:06,199
HOST: Portugal protested. Spain had sailed into waters Portugal claimed. So in 1494, Spain and Portugal drew a line through the Atlantic to settle it. The treaty read, in effect:

9
00:03:06,199 --> 00:03:16,199
HERALD: All lands east of this line: Portugal. All lands west: Spain. What each finds there, each may claim.

10
00:03:16,199 --> 00:03:54,000
HOST: So why did they sail? The textbooks answer: gold, God, and glory.  Rank the three. Which one actually paid for the ships?  Gold. Follow the money. Portugal spent fifty years mapping Africa for trade, not converts. Columbus promised the East's treasures. Spain and Portugal divided the planet by treaty to lock up their claims.  God mattered. The crusading zeal was real, and missionaries sailed with every flag. Glory mattered. Crowns competed, and explorers won titles. But neither launched one ship without the promise of profit. Gold first. God and glory rode along.

11
00:03:54,000 --> 00:04:04,000
